{"kind":"request","text":"Review request, round 4: scale evidence re-measured at main adda28c8, head 18bbedf4. Five of six child requests merged; verdicts re-derived from fresh measurements.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:c9e9ab11b7ba41875e129ac4b846a2f1bc6f3d58","conditions":"Independent review of the scale-evidence page at exact head 18bbedf4c329a980d7497ff5773b6690866c486d on request/projection-scale-stress, governed by request 5056c208 and the reporting artifact named here, which under the current lifecycle is also the implementation report. This is round 4. Rounds 1 to 3 were reviewed or re-cut against a moving main; five of the six bounded requests this work raised have since merged, so the page has been re-measured end to end at main adda28c8 rather than edited. Both envelope depths are now measured directly, not extrapolated. The questions worth your attention. First, the flipped verdicts: cold verify, checkpoint blob, warm status and PREVIEW memory all moved from miss or not-established to pass, and each names the merge that moved it — check that each attribution is right and that no pass is claimed on a slope rather than a measurement. Second, the warm-status framing: ordinary status now goes through a bounded route that passes both envelopes at 15.7 ms and 103.3 ms with a response near 730 B at any depth, while the whole-projection route is recorded as not applicable rather than as a miss because it is an explicit uncapped opt-in; judge whether that is the honest reading or whether it should stay a miss. Third, and most important, the one finding the merges did not move: at the same depth 512, restarting from a checkpoint with a 255-record tail takes 11.95 s while a full cold audit of the whole repository takes 0.30 s, both reproducible to 0.2 percent across repeated runs on a quiet machine, so a checkpoint makes restart about 40 times slower and only begins to pay near 47,000 records. I was briefed that the checkpoint-tail request was already satisfied with a maximum restart of 1.28 s; my measurements do not reproduce that, and I have recorded what I measured rather than the briefing. Please check that comparison specifically — if it is right, the checkpoint refresh cadence has an open problem at preview scale and cold restart honestly misses PREVIEW at 11.95 s against 10 s. Fourth, resident memory at FIRST-PRODUCTION remains an honest miss at 9.18 GB against 4 GiB. Two figures are still extrapolated and say so in place: restart from a checkpoint at FIRST-PRODUCTION depth, because every contract checkpoint case puts its checkpoint at depth 257 so a near-head checkpoint at 500,000 does not exist in any fixture; and envelope response sizes, taken from this repository's real 1,930 B per record rather than the fixture's inflated 6.4 KB. Record the three required conclusions. Architecture: documentation only, no layer contract change, indexed in docs/README.md, published at the maintained docs granularity, and distinguishing measured envelopes from the enforced bounds in limits.md. Security: no runtime, key, signature, secret, authority or failure-mode boundary changes; the published machine and load context carries no secret. Simplification: say whether any section earns its length, particularly the what-flipped section. Gates at this exact head: go build, go vet, go test ./internal/docset -count=1 in 95.6 s, and go test ./... -count=1 all pass — the root README public-surface failure from earlier rounds is fixed on main and the tree is green. Approval authorizes claude to merge with step-5 succession, retiring the live artifact at path docs and publishing the successor there.","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}